# 3. Methodology

## 3.1 Target Structural Modeling

### 3.1.1 Data Sourcing

The target protein sequence of the multidrug resistance efflux transporter MexB from *Pseudomonas aeruginosa* was retrieved from the [Comprehensive Antibiotic Resistance Database (CARD)](https://card.mcmaster.ca/). MexB was selected as the primary target because of its role in the Resistance-Nodulation-Division (RND) family of multidrug efflux pumps (Du et al., 2015).

The sequence retrieval procedure involved the following steps:

1. Accessed the CARD homepage.
2. Searched for MexB using the search bar.
3. Selected the corresponding entry associated with its Antibiotic Resistance Ontology (ARO) classification.
4. Navigated to the Reference Sequences section of the gene entry.
5. Retrieved the FASTA-formatted MexB protein sequence for subsequent structural analysis.

### 3.1.2 Conformational Sampling

The RND efflux pump transport mechanism involves three asymmetric functional conformations:

- **Access/Loose (L):** Initial substrate recognition and entry.
- **Binding/Tight (T):** Substrate binding within the porter domain.
- **Extrusion/Open (O):** Substrate release from the transporter.

To investigate the structural changes associated with the functional transport cycle, the three conformational states of MexB were examined (Murakami et al., 2006).

The following Protein Data Bank (PDB) structures were identified for structural analysis (Berman et al., 2000):

| Protein | Organism | PDB ID |
|---|---|---|
| MexB multidrug resistance protein | *P. aeruginosa* PAO1 | [3W9I](https://www.rcsb.org/structure/3W9I) |
| MexB multidrug resistance protein | *P. aeruginosa* PAO1 | [3W9J](https://www.rcsb.org/structure/3W9J) |

## 3.2 Selection and Preparation of the MexB Protein Structure

The three-dimensional structure of the MexB multidrug efflux transporter was obtained from the [Protein Data Bank (PDB)](https://www.rcsb.org/) using PDB ID **3W9I**.

The biological assembly containing the MexB homotrimer was selected for subsequent structural analysis. The three protomers were assigned according to their respective conformational states:

| Chain | Conformational state |
|---|---|
| A | Access |
| B | Binding |
| C | Extrusion |

The biological assembly was inspected and processed to generate suitable coordinate files for computational analysis. Each protomer was retained separately to facilitate the comparison of structural changes associated with the MexB functional transport cycle (Ryosuke et al., 2013).

## 3.3 Conformational-State Analysis of MexB

Structural comparison of the Access, Binding, and Extrusion conformations was performed using the transmembrane domains (TMDs) as structural alignment references.

The TMD regions were selected because they provide a relatively conserved structural framework, allowing changes in the porter domains to be examined independently of overall protomer orientation (Helen et al., 2000).

The analysis involved:

1. Identification and extraction of the TMD regions from all three protomers.
2. Structural alignment of the Binding and Extrusion states against the Access state using their corresponding TMD regions.
3. Calculation of root-mean-square deviation (RMSD) values to evaluate structural similarity following alignment.
4. Measurement of distances between selected PN1–PN2 and PC1–PC2 porter-domain regions.

The measured distances were used to characterize the relative opening and closing movements of the porter domains during the Access → Binding → Extrusion transition.

## 3.4 Identification of Functionally Relevant Residues

Functionally important residues associated with the MexB proton-relay mechanism were examined to ensure that the relevant transmembrane regions were retained in the structural model.

Particular attention was given to the following residues:

- ASP407
- ASP408
- LYS939
- THR976

These residues are associated with proton translocation and the energy-dependent transport mechanism of the RND efflux pump (Satoshi et al., 2006).

Additionally, the phenylalanine-rich regions of the porter domain were examined because aromatic residues may contribute to ligand recognition and stabilization within the internal binding cavities.

## 3.5 Identification of Ligand-Binding Pockets Using P2Rank

Potential ligand-binding pockets within the MexB structure were identified using **P2Rank v2.5.1**, a machine-learning-based binding-site prediction tool (Radoslav et al., 2018).

The aligned MexB conformational structures were submitted for pocket prediction. The resulting pocket predictions were evaluated using:

- Pocket prediction scores.
- Predicted binding probabilities.
- Pocket coordinates.
- Associated amino acid residues.
- Spatial location within the porter domain.

Based on the predicted pocket location and its structural position within the porter domain, the **Deep Binding Pocket (DBP)** of the Binding-state protomer (Chain B) was selected as the principal docking site for ligand screening.

## 3.6 Definition of the Deep Binding Pocket

The Deep Binding Pocket was defined using the coordinates of the P2Rank-predicted pocket.

A molecular docking grid was constructed around the predicted DBP to encompass the pocket and its surrounding ligand-accessible region (Jerome et al., 2021).

The following AutoDock Vina docking parameters were established:

| Parameter | Value |
|---|---:|
| Center X | -11.731 |
| Center Y | -0.815 |
| Center Z | -28.409 |
| Size X | 32 Å |
| Size Y | 28 Å |
| Size Z | 28 Å |
| Exhaustiveness | 16 |
| Number of modes | 9 |
| Energy range | 3 kcal/mol |
| Random seed | 42 |

The same docking grid coordinates and parameters were maintained throughout candidate ligand docking and reference-ligand validation to ensure consistency.

## 3.7 Selection of DBP Core Residues

Based on the structural location of the predicted DBP and its phenylalanine-rich binding environment, six amino acid residues were selected as the core residues for subsequent ligand interaction analysis (Jerome et al., 2021).

The selected residues were:

| S. No. | Amino acid residue |
|---|---|
| 1 | VAL139 |
| 2 | PHE178 |
| 3 | TYR327 |
| 4 | PHE573 |
| 5 | PHE610 |
| 6 | PHE628 |

These residues were used as structural markers to evaluate whether the docked ligands occupied the intended Deep Binding Pocket.

## 3.8 Ligand Dataset Preparation

A dataset consisting of candidate compounds for MexB-related ligand screening was assembled.

The ligand preparation workflow involved:

1. Retrieval of candidate ligand structures.
2. Generation or preparation of three-dimensional molecular structures.
3. Assignment of molecular properties required for docking.
4. Conversion of the prepared ligand structures into PDBQT format.
5. Generation of individual PDBQT files for molecular docking.

A total of **30 candidate ligands** were prepared and subjected to molecular docking against the predefined MexB Deep Binding Pocket (Jerome et al., 2021).

## 3.9 Molecular Docking of Candidate Ligands

Molecular docking was performed using **AutoDock Vina v1.2.7**.

Each of the 30 candidate ligands was independently docked into the predefined Deep Binding Pocket of the MexB Binding-state structure (Chain B).

The same receptor structure and docking parameters were maintained for all candidate compounds, including:

- Docking grid coordinates.
- Grid dimensions.
- Exhaustiveness.
- Number of output modes.
- Energy range.
- Random seed.

Multiple potential binding poses were generated for each ligand. The pose with the most favorable predicted binding affinity, represented by the lowest (most negative) Vina binding energy, was selected as the primary pose for subsequent structural interaction analysis (Jerome et al., 2021).

## 3.10 Preliminary Computational Ranking of Candidate Ligands

The 30 docked compounds were initially ranked according to their predicted AutoDock Vina binding affinities.

Compounds exhibiting more favorable docking scores were prioritized for detailed structural interaction analysis.

The five highest-ranking computational candidates were:

| Rank | Ligand ID | Vina Binding Energy (kcal/mol) |
|---:|---|---:|
| 1 | CHEMBL254328 | -12.90 |
| 2 | CHEMBL1170 | -11.72 |
| 3 | CHEMBL1200374 | -11.25 |
| 4 | CHEMBL2107826 | -10.98 |
| 5 | CHEMBL1336 | -10.44 |

These five compounds were subsequently examined for their interactions with the six predefined DBP core residues.

## 3.11 Core-Residue Contact Analysis

To determine whether the selected ligands occupied the intended Deep Binding Pocket rather than an alternative receptor region, ligand–residue distances were calculated for the six predefined DBP core residues.

The minimum heavy-atom distance between each ligand and each core residue was determined.

The following residues were included:

- VAL139
- PHE178
- TYR327
- PHE573
- PHE610
- PHE628

A distance threshold of **4 Å** was applied to identify close ligand–residue contacts.

Residues with minimum ligand–residue distances within 4 Å were classified as close-contact residues.

Aromatic residues exhibiting close contacts were additionally flagged as potential aromatic-contact candidates. This classification was used as a geometric descriptor and was not considered definitive evidence of specific π–π interactions (Jerome et al., 2021).

## 3.12 Validation Using Experimentally Characterized Reference Ligands

To evaluate whether the selected docking protocol could reproduce the interaction environment associated with experimentally characterized MexB-related compounds, four reference ligands were selected.

The reference compounds were:

1. CHEMBL191703
2. CHEMBL297453
3. CHEMBL4064721
4. CHEMBL5569750

The reference ligands were prepared in PDBQT format and docked into the same DBP docking grid using the AutoDock Vina parameters applied to the candidate compounds (Jerome et al., 2021).

Available experimentally reported activity information associated with the reference compounds was retained as a separate validation field, including minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC) and minimum effective concentration (MEC) measurements where available.

## 3.13 Structural Validation of Reference-Ligand Binding

The best-scoring docking pose of each reference ligand was analyzed against the six predefined DBP core residues.

The same 4 Å minimum-distance criterion used for candidate ligand analysis was applied to the reference compounds.

The resulting core-residue contact counts were:

| Reference Ligand | Core-Residue Contacts |
|---|---:|
| CHEMBL4064721 | 6/6 |
| CHEMBL5569750 | 6/6 |
| CHEMBL297453 | 5/6 |
| CHEMBL191703 | 4/6 |

The contact analysis provided a structural validation framework for evaluating whether the defined DBP and its six core residues could accommodate experimentally characterized reference ligands.

## 3.14 Comparative Analysis of Predicted and Reference Ligands

The candidate and reference ligands were compared using three principal computational parameters:

1. AutoDock Vina predicted binding energy.
2. Number of DBP core-residue contacts.
3. Minimum ligand–residue distances.

All five top-ranked candidate compounds demonstrated **6/6 contacts** with the predefined DBP core residues, indicating consistent occupation of the selected DBP region by their predicted docking poses.

The reference compounds demonstrated between 4/6 and 6/6 core-residue contacts, providing additional structural support for the relevance of the selected six-residue interaction region.

Experimental activity values were retained for contextual validation but were not directly equated with Vina docking scores because the experimental measurements represented different endpoints and units, including MIC and MEC (Eberhardt et al., 2021).

## 3.15 Final Candidate Identification

The final candidate set was determined by integrating predicted docking affinity with structural interaction analysis.

Compounds were prioritized based on the following criteria:

- Favorable predicted AutoDock Vina binding energy.
- Extensive interactions with the six predefined DBP core residues.
- Consistent occupation of the intended Deep Binding Pocket.
- Structural compatibility with the selected binding region.

The resulting compounds were proposed as computationally prioritized MexB Deep Binding Pocket-binding candidates for further investigation (Eberhardt et al., 2021).

