## 3.17 Computational Limitations

The present study represents a computational screening approach and does not establish experimental inhibition of the MexB efflux transporter.

AutoDock Vina binding scores represent predicted binding affinities and should not be interpreted as experimentally measured binding constants or direct evidence of inhibitory activity.

Furthermore, the docking procedure employed a relatively rigid receptor model and a single defined DBP conformation. Consequently, protein flexibility, solvent effects, induced-fit mechanisms, and conformational changes occurring throughout the MexB transport cycle may not be fully represented.

The 4 Å ligand–residue contact threshold was used as a geometric screening criterion and does not independently establish the chemical nature or energetic contribution of individual ligand–residue interactions.

Therefore, the identified compounds should be considered computationally prioritized candidates requiring further investigation through molecular dynamics simulations, detailed interaction analysis, binding free-energy calculations, and appropriate experimental assays.
